<h2 id="a">A</h2>

<h3 id="abbreviations">abbreviations, acronyms and initialisms</h3>

<span id="wg-024"></span>The first time you use an abbreviation on a page, write the full term with the abbreviation in brackets: passive infrared (PIR) sensor. Then use the abbreviation. Do not explain ones everyone knows, such as USB, PDF, LED or TV. Put the unit symbol next to the number: 21°C, 420W. If an abbreviation only matters in diagnostics, put it in a details section, not in a button label.

<h3 id="action">action</h3>

Use “action” for what Home Assistant is asked to do. Older instructions say “service”. Say “action”, and explain “service” only when you must refer to an old saved file. A request to run an action does not prove the device finished it. See [action](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-action).

<h3 id="active-voice">active voice</h3>

Use the active voice: “Home Assistant could not save the schedule.” Use the passive only when the thing that happened matters more than who did it: “The schedule was not saved.”

<h3 id="add-on">add-on</h3>

Home Assistant now calls these “apps”. Write “app (previously add-on)” once on a page, then “app”. See [app](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-app).

<h3 id="alarm">alarm</h3>

Use “alarm” only for a real alarm, such as a smoke alarm or a security alarm that has sounded. A reminder, a warning or a low battery is not an alarm. Too many urgent messages teach people to ignore them all. See [Choose urgency from evidence](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-042).

<h3 id="alert">alert</h3>

An alert stays on screen until the problem is gone. Use it for a fault the person should know about. A message that disappears is a toast, not an alert. See [Choose a toast, an alert or a repair](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-042).

<h3 id="alternative-text">alternative text</h3>

Write “alternative text” in full the first time, then “alt text”. Describe what the image shows that the person needs. Leave it empty for a decorative image. Do not start with “image of”. See [Images](../formatting-content/images.html#wg-082).

<h3 id="am-and-pm">am and pm</h3>

Use lower case with no space and no full stops: 5:30pm. Write “midnight” and “midday”, not 12am or 12pm. If the household uses a 24-hour clock, write 17:30 and do not mix the two on one screen.

<h3 id="ampersand">ampersand</h3>

Use “and”, not “&”. Keep an ampersand only inside a name someone typed or a native Home Assistant label, such as **Automations & scenes**.

<h3 id="apostrophes">apostrophes</h3>

Use a curly apostrophe: Nain’s lamp. Do not use one to make a plural: 3 lights, not 3 light’s. Never change an apostrophe inside a name someone typed or inside code.

<h3 id="app">app</h3>

Use “app” for software that runs alongside Home Assistant on Home Assistant OS. Do not use it for an integration or a dashboard card. See [app](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-app).

<h3 id="area">area</h3>

A room or other space that groups devices. Write the name the household gave it: Kitchen, Back bedroom. An area is not a zone. See [area](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-area).

<h3 id="assist">Assist</h3>

Capital A. Home Assistant’s voice and text assistant. Do not use “Siri”, “Alexa” or “OK Google” as a general word for a voice assistant. See [Assist](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-assist-language-assist).

<h3 id="automation">automation</h3>

Lower case. An automation starts when a trigger happens and then runs its actions. Say whether it is saved, turned on or running now, because those are three different facts. See [Keep saved, enabled and running apart](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-035).

<h3 id="away">Away</h3>

Capital A when it is the label a presence tracker reports. It describes a phone or tracker, not a person. Write “Alex’s phone last reported Away at 5:40pm”, not “Alex is out”. See [Treat presence as sensitive and uncertain](../writing-guidelines/right-tone.html#wg-066).

<h2 id="b">B</h2>

<h3 id="battery">battery</h3>

Show the level with its unit: Battery 12%. Say what the sensor reported and when. Do not write “battery dead” unless the device says it has no power. “Replace the battery” is an instruction; “Battery low” is a state.

<h3 id="bold">bold</h3>

Use bold only for a control the person must select or a label they will see on screen: Select **Save**. Do not use bold for emphasis. The one exception is a single critical word in a safety step. See [Write a warning](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-042).

<h3 id="brackets">brackets</h3>

Use brackets for an abbreviation after the full term and for a short, necessary note. Do not put a whole sentence in brackets. Never use square brackets in prose.

<h3 id="british-sign-language-bsl">British Sign Language (BSL)</h3>

Write “British Sign Language (BSL)” the first time. Do not call a written transcript a sign language version. Do not show a BSL video link until a signed video exists. See [Attachments](../formatting-content/attachments.html#wg-064).

<h3 id="bullet-points">bullet points</h3>

See [Text formatting](../formatting-content/text-formatting.html).

<h3 id="button">button</h3>

Name a button with a verb and its object: Turn on reading lamp. A button asks for something. It does not report what happened. Do not use “Submit”, “OK” or “Yes”. See [Write clear titles](../writing-guidelines/clear-titles.html).

<h2 id="c">C</h2>

<h3 id="camera">camera</h3>

Say when a picture was taken: Snapshot from 9:06am. Do not label an old snapshot “Live”. Name the camera by where it points: Front door camera.

<h3 id="capital-letters">capital letters and punctuation</h3>

<span id="wg-023"></span>Use sentence case for headings, labels and buttons: Turn on reading lamp. Use capitals only for the first word, names and product names. Do not write whole sentences in capitals. End a full sentence with a full stop. Do not end a heading, label or button with one.

<h3 id="card">card</h3>

A block on a dashboard. Do not call it a “widget” or “tile” unless that is the card’s name. Write civica card names in sentence case. See [card](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-dashboard-language-card).

<h3 id="card-mod">card-mod</h3>

Lower case, with a hyphen. It is a separate custom integration. Say so when a civica card needs it. Do not tell someone to install it without saying why.

<h3 id="celsius">Celsius</h3>

Write 21°C, with no space. Say “degrees Celsius” only when you write for speech. Use Fahrenheit only when the household has chosen it in Home Assistant. Do not convert a reading yourself.

<h3 id="charts">charts</h3>

Give every chart a title, units, the period it covers and a table or sentence with the same facts. See [Charts and data](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html).

<h3 id="click">click</h3>

Use “select”, not “click”. People use touch, keyboards, voice and switches as well as mice. Do not use “tap” unless you are writing only for a phone screen.

<h3 id="clocks-changing">clocks changing</h3>

Say which day a schedule means when the clocks change. On Sunday 25 October 2026 the hour from 1am to 2am happens twice. A schedule at 1:30am may run once, so say which. Use “British Summer Time (BST)” and “Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)” in full the first time.

<h3 id="colon">colon</h3>

Use a colon to introduce a list or an example. Do not use one in a heading or a label. Do not use a colon and a dash together.

<h3 id="colour">colour</h3>

Use UK spelling in your own words. Keep a native Home Assistant label as it is, even when it says “Color”. Do not rely on colour alone to show a state: say it in words as well. See [Do not depend on colour or position](../formatting-content/text-formatting.html#wg-081).

<h3 id="commas">commas</h3>

Do not use a comma before “and” in a list unless it avoids confusion. Use a comma after an introductory phrase: If the sensor reports Closed, the heating stays on. Do not put a comma between a month and a year: 2 June 2026.

<h3 id="contractions">contractions</h3>

In warnings, conditions and short instructions, write “cannot” and “do not”, not “can’t” and “don’t”. Elsewhere “you’ll” and “it’s” are fine. Pick one form for each word and keep it on one screen. See [Write important conditions in full](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-014).

<h2 id="d">D</h2>

<h3 id="dashboard">dashboard</h3>

Lower case. A set of views and cards for checking or controlling the home. Name a dashboard by what it is for, such as Heating or Security, not by who built it. See [dashboard](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-dashboard-language-dashboard).

<h3 id="dates">dates</h3>

Write the day, then the month in full, then the year: 2 June 2026. Do not use “2nd”, or numbers only (06/02/26). Use “to” in a range, not a dash: 2 June to 9 June. Show the date when a reading is more than a day old.

<h3 id="daylight-saving-time">daylight saving time</h3>

Use “British Summer Time (BST)” and “Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)”. See [clocks changing](#clocks-changing).

<h3 id="decimal-places">decimal places</h3>

Show only the digits the sensor measures. A thermometer that reads to 0.1 shows 21.5°C, not 21.50°C. Use a decimal point, not a comma. See [Keep meaningful precision](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-071).

<h3 id="device">device</h3>

A physical product or a service that groups several entities. One device may have many entities. Use the name the household gave it. Do not call every entity a device. See [device](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-device).

<h3 id="diagnostics">diagnostics</h3>

Say what a diagnostics file contains and who can see it. Tell people to check it for private details before they share it. Put the file type and size in the link: Download diagnostics (.json, 140KB). See [Attachments](../formatting-content/attachments.html).

<h3 id="disabled">disabled</h3>

Avoid. In Home Assistant it can mean turned off, hidden or unavailable. Say which: “The automation is turned off.” “The entity is hidden.” Do not use “disabled” about people.

<h3 id="do-not">do not</h3>

See [contractions](#contractions).

<h2 id="e">E</h2>

<h3 id="e-g">e.g.</h3>

Use “for example”, “such as” or “including”. Do not use “e.g.”, “i.e.” or “etc.”. Never write “etc.” after “for example”.

<h3 id="email">email</h3>

One word, lower case. Write “email address” when you mean the address. Do not put an email address in a notification preview.

<h3 id="entity">entity</h3>

One reading, control or function in Home Assistant, such as a lamp or a temperature. Say “light”, “sensor” or “switch” when you can. An entity is not always a physical device. See [entity](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-entity).

<h3 id="entity-id">entity ID</h3>

Write “entity ID”, not “entity id” or “identifier”. Put the ID in code formatting when someone must copy it: `light.reading_lamp`. Label an example ID as an example. Changing the name of an entity does not necessarily change its ID.

<h3 id="error-messages">error messages</h3>

Say what happened, then what the person can do. Name the confirmed cause. Say if the fault is the system’s. Do not blame the person or the device. Put a message you refer to in quotation marks and do not bold it. See [Give a recovery that matches the cause](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-040).

<h3 id="exclamation-marks">exclamation marks</h3>

Do not use them. They sound like panic or like a sales pitch. Do not celebrate routine actions: “Light on” is enough.

<h2 id="f">F</h2>

<h3 id="fahrenheit">Fahrenheit</h3>

Write 70°F only when the household has chosen Fahrenheit in Home Assistant. See [Celsius](#celsius).

<h3 id="firmware">firmware</h3>

The software inside a device. Say “update the firmware” only when the device’s own maker uses the word. Otherwise say “update the device”. Do not promise an update will fix a fault unless the maker says it does.

<h3 id="floor">floor</h3>

A group of areas on one level. Do not guess a building layout from it. See [floor](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-floor).

<h3 id="full-stops">full stops</h3>

Do not use them at the end of headings, labels, buttons or table cells that are not sentences. Use them at the end of every full sentence, including a hint under a field. Do not use them in abbreviations: UK, kWh.

<h2 id="g">G</h2>

<h3 id="gateway">gateway</h3>

Use the name printed on the device or in its app, such as “hub” or “bridge”. Do not use “gateway” and “hub” for the same device on one page.

<h3 id="gb-mb-kb">GB, MB, kB</h3>

Write 140kB and 2GB with no space. Use these only when the size matters, such as a download or a backup.

<h3 id="google-home-and-amazon-alexa">Google Home and Amazon Alexa</h3>

Use the product name exactly as the maker writes it. Do not use “Alexa” or “Google” as a general word for a voice assistant. Say what the integration can and cannot do. Do not say a device “works with” one unless the maker says so.

<h2 id="h">H</h2>

<h3 id="headings">headings</h3>

Use sentence case. Start with a verb where you can: Pair a motion sensor. Do not use a question or “Introduction”. Do not skip a heading level.

<h3 id="heating">heating</h3>

“Heating on” is a state. “Turn on heating” is an action. Do not write “Heating on” on a button. If the boiler has not confirmed, say “Heating requested”. See [Separate state from action](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-031).

<h3 id="home-assistant">Home Assistant</h3>

Write in full the first time on a page. Do not use “HA” in text people read. Do not write “HomeAssistant” or “Hass”. Keep native labels and the names people gave their devices exactly as they are.

<h3 id="household-members">household members</h3>

Avoid assumptions about who is in the home. Do not write “your family” or “your partner”. Write “someone in your home” or “a household member”. See [Do not assume who is reading](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-060).

<h3 id="hub">hub</h3>

Use the name printed on the device or in its app. If a page covers more than one hub, name each by its brand. Do not call Home Assistant itself a “hub”.

<h3 id="hyphens">hyphens</h3>

Use hyphens in compound modifiers only when they prevent confusion: a motion-triggered light. Write “wifi” without one. Keep native names as they are: Z-Wave, card-mod.

<h2 id="i">I</h2>

<h3 id="icons">icons</h3>

Do not use an icon on its own. Add a visible text label, or give the icon a text alternative. An icon that shows a state must also say the state in words. See [Do not depend on colour or position](../formatting-content/text-formatting.html#wg-081).

<h3 id="i-e">i.e.</h3>

See [e.g.](#e-g).

<h3 id="integration">integration</h3>

The connection between Home Assistant and a device family or service. Not the same as an app. Say “the Zigbee integration” only when a person has to find it in Home Assistant. See [integration](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-integration).

<h3 id="internet">internet</h3>

Lower case. Do not say a feature “works without the internet” unless the maker says so. Say what stops working when the connection is lost.

<h3 id="ip-address">IP address</h3>

Write “IP address” in full the first time. Put the address in code formatting. Do not show it on a shared screen.

<h3 id="italics">italics</h3>

Do not use italics. Put the name of a document in “double quotation marks”.

<h2 id="j">J</h2>

<h3 id="jargon">jargon</h3>

Use the word the household uses. Keep a technical term when it is the exact label on screen, and explain it the first time. Do not swap a precise word for a comforting one that is wrong. See [Use familiar, concrete words](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-010).

<h2 id="k">K</h2>

<h3 id="keyboard">keyboard</h3>

Do not assume a keyboard or a mouse. Say “select”. Write key names in bold: press **Enter**.

<h3 id="kilowatts-and-kilowatt-hours">kilowatts and kilowatt-hours</h3>

Power is a rate at a moment, in kilowatts (kW) or watts (W). Energy is an amount over a period, in kilowatt-hours (kWh). Write 420W and 4.62kWh with no space. Never write “kW/h”. Say which one you mean. See [Name the measured quantity](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-070).

<h2 id="l">L</h2>

<h3 id="labels">labels</h3>

Name a control with a verb and its object. Make repeated labels different from each other: Lounge lamp settings, not Settings. Use sentence case. Keep the label Home Assistant shows for a native screen. See [Write clear titles](../writing-guidelines/clear-titles.html).

<h3 id="last-changed-last-updated-and-last-reported">last changed, last updated and last reported</h3>

Home Assistant records when a state last changed, when it was last updated and when it was last reported. These are different times. A door sensor may not change for days and still be working. Use the time the source gave and say what it means: Last reported 9:15am.

<h3 id="light">light</h3>

Use “light” for the thing and “lamp” only when it is a lamp. Do not write “bulb” for a light fitting. Name the light by where it is or what it is: Hall light, Reading lamp.

<h3 id="links">links</h3>

Make the link text describe where it goes. Do not use “click here”, “more” or a bare address. A link goes somewhere. It does not run a device action. See [Write effective links](../writing-guidelines/add-links.html).

<h3 id="lists">lists</h3>

Use a lead-in line. Start each bullet in lower case unless it is a name. Do not use “and” or “or” after bullets. Put no full stop after the last bullet. Use numbers only for steps in order. See [Text formatting](../formatting-content/text-formatting.html).

<h3 id="lock">lock</h3>

“Locked” is what the lock reports. It does not prove the door is shut. Write “The lock reports Locked.” For a button, write “Lock front door”.

<h2 id="m">M</h2>

<h3 id="measurements">measurements</h3>

Use numerals and no space before the unit: 3,500kg, 21°C, 4.62kWh. Use metric units. Never show a number without its unit. See [Keep units with their values](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-073).

<h3 id="midnight-and-midday">midnight and midday</h3>

Write “midnight” and “midday”, not 00:00, 12am, 12pm or “noon”. Midnight is the first minute of the day. For a deadline, use 11:59pm and the date.

<h3 id="mobile-phone">mobile phone</h3>

Say “phone”. Say “the Home Assistant app on your phone” when you mean the app. Do not assume a person has one.

<h3 id="months">months</h3>

Write the month in full: 2 June 2026. Use an abbreviation only in a narrow table column.

<h3 id="motion">motion</h3>

A motion sensor reports movement, not a person. Write “Motion detected at 10:12pm”. Do not write “Someone is in the hall”. Do not name the person it might be.

<h2 id="n">N</h2>

<h3 id="names">names</h3>

Keep the name a person gave a device exactly as typed: case, spelling, punctuation and language. Do not guess a relationship from it. Do not change an entity ID when someone changes a name. See [Keep names exactly as people typed them](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-022).

<h3 id="notifications">notifications</h3>

Say what was observed, when, and nothing a stranger reading a lock screen should not see. Put the device and time in the first line: Front door opened at 10:15pm. Do not put a password, a token or an exact address in one. See [Content blocks](../formatting-content/content-blocks.html#wg-045).

<h3 id="numbers">numbers</h3>

Write numbers as numerals, including 1 to 9: 3 lights are on. Write “one” only inside a phrase such as “one of the lights”. Use a comma in thousands: 1,200. Use “to” in a range: 3 to 5 lights.

<h2 id="o">O</h2>

<h3 id="offline">offline</h3>

Say “offline” only if you know the connection is lost. Otherwise use the state the source gave, such as unavailable or unknown, with a time. See [Keep unavailable, unknown, stale and offline apart](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-033).

<h3 id="ok">OK</h3>

Do not use it as a label. Say what will happen: Save schedule, Close. Do not use it to mean “working”. Say what the device reported.

<h3 id="on-and-off">on and off</h3>

“On” and “off” are states a device reports. “Turn on” and “Turn off” are actions. Do not write “switch on” or “toggle”. Write “Turn on reading lamp”, not “On”.

<h2 id="p">P</h2>

<h3 id="passwords-and-tokens">passwords and tokens</h3>

Never ask for a password or access token in a field that does not need one. Never show one in a notification, a link or a log you tell someone to share. Say how to sign in on the maker’s own site. See [Ask for personal information only when needed](../writing-guidelines/clear-structure.html#wg-061).

<h3 id="per-cent">per cent</h3>

Use the % symbol with a numeral and no space: 30%. Say “percentage points” when you compare two percentages.

<h3 id="person">person</h3>

In Home Assistant, a person combines phones and other trackers. It is not a user account and does not prove where someone is. See [person](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-domain-person).

<h3 id="phone-numbers">phone numbers</h3>

Say why you need one, who sees it and that it is optional unless it is required. Write UK numbers with spaces: 020 7946 0000. Do not put one in a notification preview.

<h3 id="please">please</h3>

Do not say “please” or “please note”. Say what to do: Check the battery.

<h3 id="power">power</h3>

Power is a rate at a moment, measured in watts (W) or kilowatts (kW). It is not energy. See [kilowatts and kilowatt-hours](#kilowatts-and-kilowatt-hours).

<h3 id="presence">presence</h3>

A tracker or sensor reports a device or movement, not a person. Do not show presence on a shared screen unless the household chose to. See [Treat presence as sensitive and uncertain](../writing-guidelines/right-tone.html#wg-066).

<h2 id="q">Q</h2>

<h3 id="question-marks">question marks</h3>

Do not use a question as a heading. Use one in a form question that asks for a decision: Send a notification when the front door opens after 10pm?

<h3 id="quotation-marks">quotation marks</h3>

Use curly double quotation marks: “Connection lost.” Put an error message you refer to in quotation marks and do not bold it. Use straight quotation marks only inside code. Never change the quotation marks inside a value someone must copy.

<h2 id="r">R</h2>

<h3 id="reading">reading</h3>

A reading is what a sensor reported at a time. Say when: 19.4°C at 9:15am. Do not call an old reading current. Do not show “No reading” as 0. See [Keep gaps and real zeros apart](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-076).

<h3 id="repair">repair</h3>

A Home Assistant repair explains what is wrong, why it matters and gives the exact steps to fix it as a numbered list. See [Choose a toast, an alert or a repair](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-042).

<h3 id="request-sent">request sent</h3>

Say “Request sent” until the device reports a result. Do not say “Done” or “Success”. See [Say a request was sent, not that it worked](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-032).

<h3 id="reset-and-restart">reset and restart</h3>

These are different. “Restart” turns a device off and on and keeps its settings. “Reset” returns it to factory settings and may remove them. Say what will be lost before the step. Do not use “reboot”.

<h3 id="retry">retry</h3>

Offer “Try again” only when trying again is safe and will not repeat the action. If the first request may still complete, say so before the person retries.

<h2 id="s">S</h2>

<h3 id="saved-sent-and-selected">saved, sent and selected</h3>

Say which. “Saved” means the setting is stored. “Sent” means the request left. “Selected” means the person chose it. None of them proves the device did anything.

<h3 id="scene">scene</h3>

A saved set of device states you can request together. A scene is not a timed routine. Activating one does not prove every device reached its state. See [scene](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-scene).

<h3 id="schedule">schedule</h3>

Say what it does and when: Heating on at 6:30am on weekdays. Say which days and which clock. Say what happens if a device cannot be reached.

<h3 id="select">select</h3>

Use “select” for choosing any control. Do not use “click”, “tap”, “press” or “hit”. Use “turn on” for a toggle: Turn on **Porch light at sunset**.

<h3 id="sensor">sensor</h3>

A device that reports a reading. Name it by what it measures and where: Hall temperature, Back door contact sensor. A sensor reports. It does not decide. See [sensor](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-sensor).

<h3 id="settings">settings</h3>

Write **Settings** in bold when you refer to the Home Assistant screen. Write “settings” in lower case for choices in general. Do not write “config” or “configuration” for what a household would call settings.

<h3 id="signal-strength">signal strength</h3>

Say what it means for the person: Weak signal. Move the hub closer. Do not show a raw number such as -87dBm unless the task is diagnostics.

<h3 id="smart">smart</h3>

Avoid “smart” as a label for what a device does. Say what it does: Heating adjusts to your schedule. Use “smart” only inside a product name.

<h3 id="sound">sound</h3>

Do not rely on sound alone. Say it in words too. For an alarm sound, write what the sound means and show a visible message as well. See [Do not assume one way to see, hear or act](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-063).

<h3 id="states">states</h3>

A state is the value Home Assistant holds for an entity. It is not necessarily a fresh reading or what a person asked for. Say what the device reported, with a time. See [Separate state from action](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-031).

<h3 id="sunrise-and-sunset">sunrise and sunset</h3>

Say the time and the place: Sunset in Cardiff, 4:32pm. Say “sunrise” and “sunset”, not “dusk” or “dawn”, unless the automation uses those.

<h2 id="t">T</h2>

<h3 id="temperature">temperature</h3>

Write the reading, the unit and the time: 19.4°C at 9:15am. Say “target” for what the household set and “current” for what the sensor reported. Do not call the target “the temperature”.

<h3 id="text-message">text message</h3>

Write “text message”, not “SMS” or “text”. Say what will be sent and to whom before the person agrees.

<h3 id="time-zones">time zones</h3>

Say the time zone when a time could be misread: 10:00 on 11 November 2026 (Europe/London). Use the household’s time zone. Do not assume the zone of the viewer.

<h3 id="times">times</h3>

Use the household’s clock setting and keep to it on one screen. On a 12-hour clock write 5:30pm. On a 24-hour clock write 17:30. Never write 1730hrs. Use “to” in a range: 10am to 11am. Write “midnight” and “midday”. See [Make ambiguous times explicit](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-075).

<h3 id="title-case">title case</h3>

Do not use it. Write headings and labels in sentence case: Turn on reading lamp, not Turn On Reading Lamp.

<h3 id="toast">toast</h3>

A toast is a short message that disappears. Use it only for feedback the person does not need to act on. Never put something in a toast that someone must read to fix a problem. See [Choose a toast, an alert or a repair](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-042).

<h3 id="turn-on-and-turn-off">turn on and turn off</h3>

Use “turn on” and “turn off” for lights, heating and appliances. Do not use “switch on”, “power up”, “activate” or “toggle”. Name the thing: Turn off kitchen lights.

<h2 id="u">U</h2>

<h3 id="unavailable-and-unknown">unavailable and unknown</h3>

“Unavailable” means Home Assistant cannot get the state now. “Unknown” means no usable state has been supplied. Neither means 0, and neither proves the device is offline. See [unavailable](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-state-records-unavailable) in A to Z Technical.

<h3 id="undo">undo</h3>

Offer **Undo** only when the system can really undo the action. Say what it will do: Undo turning off hall light. Do not offer it after an action that cannot be reversed.

<h3 id="units">units</h3>

Put the unit straight after the number with no space: 21°C, 4.62kWh. Never show a number without its unit. Use the symbol the source uses. See [measurements](#measurements).

<h3 id="update">update</h3>

“Update” is both an action and a time. Say “Update the hub” for the action. Say “Page last changed 9 November 2026” for when writing changed, and “Last reported 9:15am” for a device reading. Never show one as the other.

<h3 id="url">URL</h3>

Write “web address” in text people read. Put a full address in code formatting only when someone must copy it. Do not show an address with a token or password in it.

<h3 id="usb">USB</h3>

Write “USB” with no expansion. Write “USB stick” or “USB cable”, not “USB drive” unless the maker does.

<h2 id="v">V</h2>

<h3 id="version-numbers">version numbers</h3>

Write Home Assistant versions as the maker does: 2026.11.1. Say which version a screen or label was checked against. Do not say a feature works in a home without checking its version.

<h3 id="voice">voice</h3>

Use “voice” for speaking to Assist. Say what Assist heard and did. Do not say it “understood” unless it reports a result. See [Assist](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-assist-language-assist).

<h3 id="voltage">voltage</h3>

Write 230V with no space. Never ask a household to change mains wiring. Link to the maker’s manual for a safety step. See [Do not assume who is reading](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-060).

<h2 id="w">W</h2>

<h3 id="warnings">warnings</h3>

Put a warning before the step it affects. Say the hazard, what happens and what to do. Use “Danger”, “Warning” and “Caution” only for a risk to people. See [Write a warning](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-042).

<h3 id="weather">weather</h3>

Say where and when: Rain in Swansea at 3pm. A forecast is not a reading. Do not say it “will” rain. Say “forecast” or “reported”.

<h3 id="welsh">Welsh</h3>

Write English in English and Welsh in Welsh. Ask a Welsh speaker to write the Welsh. Do not machine-translate and publish. Do not mix languages in one control. See [Preserve language and translation](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-065).

<h3 id="wifi">wifi</h3>

Write “wifi” in lower case, with no hyphen, unless you quote a native label that spells it differently. Name the network exactly as the household typed it.

<h3 id="words-to-avoid">words to avoid</h3>

Do not use “simply”, “just”, “easy”, “obviously” or “please”. They make a step sound easy for the writer and hard for the reader. Say what to do.

<h2 id="x">X</h2>

<h3 id="x-axis-and-y-axis">x-axis and y-axis</h3>

Write “x-axis” and “y-axis” in lower case with a hyphen. Label each axis with what it measures and its unit: Energy (kWh), Time (hour). See [Give charts a text equivalent](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-077).

<h2 id="y">Y</h2>

<h3 id="years">years</h3>

Write the full year: 2026. Do not use an apostrophe: the 1990s, not the 1990’s. Do not write “this year” in content that will last.

<h3 id="you-and-your">you and your</h3>

Say “you” and “your” when it helps the person: Your heating is on. Do not use “the user”, “the end user” or “users”. Use “we” only when it is clear who “we” is. Software is not a person watching the house.

<h2 id="z">Z</h2>

<h3 id="zigbee">Zigbee</h3>

Capital Z. Zigbee devices pass messages through each other. Plug in a mains-powered Zigbee device between the hub and a sensor that cannot reach it. Do not write “ZigBee” or “zigbee”. See also Z-Wave.

<h3 id="zone">zone</h3>

A place on a map used for location features. A zone is not a room. A phone entering a zone does not prove a person is there. See [zone](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-zone).

<h3 id="z-wave">Z-Wave</h3>

Capital Z and W with a hyphen. Do not write “Zwave” or “Z wave”. Name the integration that supports it.

